[[_getting_git_on_a_server]]
=== Getting Git on a Server
tag::concept[]

In order to initially set up any Git server, you have to export an existing repository into a new **bare repository**—a repository that doesn’t contain a working directory.

Think of a normal repository (like Nora's `sketchspark` folder) as a "working studio": it has the history (the `.git` folder) and all the files she is currently editing. A **bare** repository is just the history. It's the "central brain" of the project, intended only for sharing and backups. Conventionally, bare repository directory names end with the suffix `.git`.

==== Creating a Bare Repository
tag::procedure[]

(((git commands, clone, bare)))
To create a bare version of her project, Nora uses the `--bare` option with the `clone` command:

[source,bash]
----
$ git clone --bare sketchspark sketchspark.git
Cloning into bare repository 'sketchspark.git'...
done.
----

Nora now has a directory named `sketchspark.git` that contains only the Git data. This is what she will put on the team's server.

[[_bare_repo]]
==== Putting the Bare Repository on a Server
tag::procedure[]

Suppose Nora has a small Linux server she uses for internal tools, reachable at `git.sketchspark.io`. She has SSH access to this server and wants to store the repo under `/srv/git`.

First, she copies her bare repository to the server:

[source,bash]
----
$ scp -r sketchspark.git nora@git.sketchspark.io:/srv/git
----

At this point, Sam or Priya can clone the repository to their own machines:

[source,bash]
----
$ git clone nora@git.sketchspark.io:/srv/git/sketchspark.git
----

If Nora wants to make sure the file permissions are set correctly for a shared team, she can log into the server and run `git init --shared`:

[source,bash]
----
$ ssh nora@git.sketchspark.io
$ cd /srv/git/sketchspark.git
$ git init --bare --shared
----

This ensures that anyone in the team's group can read and write to the repository.

As you can see, setting up a "Git Server" can be as simple as having an SSH account and a bare repository. There is no special "Git software" to install on the server—just Git itself. For a small team of designers, this might be all you ever need. However, as Nora will soon discover, hosted options like GitHub offer much more than just a place to store files.
